memo('ethics','About this fictional training study','All interviews, residents, coding decisions and findings are invented for software testing and video production. Settings are illustrative, not representative of countries or communities. No real interviews, consent records or climate measurements were collected. Show the fictional-data label in the video.','methodological');
memo('question','Research question and analytic orientation','How do residents describe the links between environmental disruption, everyday work, care, public support and decisions to stay or move? Use deductive starter themes and allow interpretation to refine them. Compare mechanisms and contrasts; do not infer prevalence from four scripted cases.','methodological',residents.map(r=>r.id));
memo('protocol','Three-coder protocol and intentional differences','Uneven sentence-level selections include single-coded, overlapping and uncoded sentences; explicit event-phase tags are researcher-defined for training, not inferred from import dates. Amina, Ravi and Lina are simulated coders, not separate authenticated app accounts. All three reviewed all four transcripts. Q1, Q5 and Q12 use a single shared code for clear agreement examples. Q2 changes Lina\'s selection boundaries. Q8 swaps care for income in Ravi\'s coding. Q9 swaps voice for eligibility in Lina\'s coding. Q11 omits mobility for Ravi. Lina intentionally misses Nepal Q12 and omits debt in Bangladesh Q4. Export ICR before adjudication and preserve a copy of the original project.','methodological',residents.map(r=>r.id));
memo('water','Findings draft — water access is also time','Across these scripted cases, water access depends on routes, power, storage, queues and available carers. Mere and Nasima describe water collection displacing income-generating work. Arun connects water repair with clinic access. Sari emphasises storage and electricity. Working claim: service disruption travels through everyday time and care. Use linked excerpts as evidence; this is an illustrative finding, not an empirical result.','analytic',residents.map(r=>r.id),['services','water','care'],residents.map(r=>[r.id,3]));
memo('invisible','Discussion prompt — resilience can conceal effort','Q8 makes invisible care and cleaning visible. Compare appreciative descriptions of resilience with who absorbs unpaid work. Counterpoint: interviewees also value mutual support, so avoid writing as if community action is only a burden. The draft should hold practical solidarity and unequal effort together.','analytic',residents.map(r=>r.id),['services','care','mutual'],residents.map(r=>[r.id,8]));
memo('mobility','Findings draft — relocation is more than an address','All four fictional residents weigh safety against ties, income, care and cost. Nasima describes a possible partial household move; Arun considers seasonal work; Mere and Sari compare higher-ground access with lost networks. Contrast temporary, partial and permanent mobility instead of collapsing them into one decision.','analytic',residents.map(r=>r.id),['belonging','mobility','place'],residents.map(r=>[r.id,11]));
memo('fairness','Findings draft — support can create its own barriers','Nasima describes document replacement and transport costs. Sari describes ownership-based access and tenant authority. Mere and Arun discuss whose priorities count. Compare administrative eligibility with participation: they can overlap, but they answer different analytic questions. Review the Q9 disagreement before refining code definitions.','analytic',residents.map(r=>r.id),['governance','voice','eligibility'],residents.map(r=>[r.id,9]));
memo('negative','Contrast memo — mutual aid is not automatically inclusive','Q7 shows effective informal support, alongside people missed because they are new, rent rooms, are not in a phone group, or cannot contribute labour. Nasima\'s account includes an active invitation to newcomers, offering a contrast to exclusion rather than another identical example.','analytic',residents.map(r=>r.id),['agency','mutual'],residents.map(r=>[r.id,7]));
memo('adjudication','Consensus meeting agenda — care, income and voice','Start with Nasima Q8: discuss whether unpaid care is explicit and whether income is an additional code rather than a replacement. Then examine Sari Q9: separate ownership eligibility from resident voice. Finally inspect Mere Q2: agree whether event and aftermath belong in one coding span. Decisions are intentionally unresolved so the video can demonstrate adjudication.','methodological',['bangladesh','indonesia','fiji'],[],[['bangladesh',8],['indonesia',9],['fiji',2]]);
memo('journal','Research journal — from codes to a report','Demo session plan: read a source; inspect code definitions; filter coding by Amina; compare cases in the framework; open relationship memos; use Memo-Book to select writing material; export a manuscript skeleton; then compare ICR and consensus before making any adjudication changes. Record how a code definition changes after a disagreement.','journal');
