write('01_questionnaire.md',`# Fictional interview questionnaire\n\nPurpose: explore how environmental disruption affects work, services, care, support and mobility. All material is invented for eQc testing and video demonstration. Estimated real-world interview length would be 30–45 minutes; these scripts are shorter.\n\n## Opening script\n\nFor a real study, explain purpose, voluntary participation, recording, confidentiality, skipping questions and stopping; obtain appropriate consent before interviewing. This dummy package has no real consent or participants. Use participant aliases and avoid collecting precise home locations.\n\n${questions.map((q,i)=>`## Q${i+1}. ${q[0]}\n\n${q[1]}\n\nOptional probes: ${q[2]}\n`).join('\n')}\n## Closing\n\nThank the participant and ask whether anything should be corrected or excluded. In this fictional exercise, discuss how interviewing choices influence later coding.\n`);
write('02_codebook.md',`# Starter codebook — fictional climate resilience study\n\nVersion: training draft 1. Codes describe participants' scripted accounts; they do not establish climate causation. Root themes and child codes may both be applied when appropriate. Three coders share these definitions but include deliberate interpretation differences.\n\n${themes.map(t=>`## ${t[1]}\n\n${t[3]}\n\n${t.slice(4).map(c=>`### ${c[1]}\n\n${c[2]}\n`).join('\n')}`).join('\n')}\n## Coding rules\n\nCode resident speech, not interviewer questions or the fictional-data header. Retain enough context to interpret the statement. Multiple codes may overlap. Apply Mobility when movement is considered, even without a final decision. Apply Unpaid care only when care/cleaning/time is explicit; income loss can be an additional code. Distinguish participation in decisions from eligibility for assistance. Do not interpret repeated coding by three coders as three independent interviewees.\n`);
